`--conservative-match-inference` is now on by default, and `--conservative-match-inference:false` restores the earlier inference. It now also decides, by propagation and without default type decisions, that an identifier without arguments in a pattern is a bound variable when the type it matches cannot be a datatype. So, with `--type-system-refresh`, a literal in an earlier `if` branch or `match` case of a function whose result type is, for example, `Option<int32>` takes the type `int32` from that result type instead of `int`, also when the match source is built from a literal, as in `var d := Some(0); match d case Some(n) => ...` (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/48). The option adds and removes no type constraint, and the verifier still checks the newtype's constraint on each literal. It has no effect on the legacy resolver.
